# Lecture 09D

## Yeast and Unordered Tetrads

## Chapter 8

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 3, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Follow haploid and diploid stages in the yeast life cycle.
- Explain what an ascus contains.
- Explain why retaining all four meiotic products helps genetic analysis.

=== layout: definitions

# What Is a Tetrad?

@body

- Ascus: the sac containing the products of meiosis.
- Tetrad: the four haploid products of one meiosis.
- Ascospore: one haploid spore inside an ascus.

=== layout: section

# Yeast: Mating and Meiosis

=== layout: one-panel

# Products of Meiosis

@body

- With Drosophila, mice, peas, people, and other diploid organisms, each offspring represents only one of the four potential gametes generated from a meiosis.
- Thus, until now, linkage mapping has been from examining the phenotypes of diploid progeny.
  - For such diploid organisms, we do not know the connection between individuals and parental gametes.

=== layout: two-panels

# All Four Products Stay Together

@left

- In contrast, some fungi [*maintain all four haploid products of meiosis in a single sac called an ascus*]{color=#FF33FF}.
- These haploid cells survive as viable individuals that grow and perpetuate themselves by mitosis.
  - The phenotype of such haploid fungi is a direct representation of their genotype, without complications of dominance.

@right

![One ascus contains four labeled haploid spores](assets/lect09_tetrad_concepts/homework_ascus.svg)

@citation

Homework symbols: lowercase letters = mutant alleles; + = wild type. Colors identify alleles.

